  <title>4.1.5: Modeling Relationships between Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00838</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.1.5: Modeling Relationships between Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3fb32321-d0c1-42d5-b898-0ffaafadb33e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>A parent is using a garden hose to fill up a small inflatable pool for her young child. The pool has a capacity of 90 gallons. She turns the water off after 5 minutes, but she leaves the hose in the pool for another 3 minutes before putting it away.</para>
<para>In this situation, the relationship between the gallons of water in the pool and the time, in minutes, since the parent started filling the pool can be seen as a function.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>In that function, which variable is independent? </item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The time is independent.</para>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item>In that function, which variable is dependent?</item>
    <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> The amount of water is dependent.</para>  
    <item>Write a sentence of the form “___ is a function of ___.” </item>
    <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>The amount of water, in gallons, is a function of time, in minutes, since water started filling the pool.</para>
    <item>Sketch a possible graph of the relationship on the coordinate plane. Be sure to label and indicate a scale on each axis.</item>
    <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>See sample graph.</para>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Function. time in minutes and amount of water in gallons."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1f2dc89ad31e3410880fff4d74630191130928f7"/></media></figure></para>

<para>(Students were provided access to Desmos.)</para>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More Chances</title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool-down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool-down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize over the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para></section>
